const SECTION_1_SCRIPT = `International English Language Testing System. Listening Practice Simulation.
Section 1. You will hear a telephone conversation between an eco-sanctuary reservations manager and a university research team director.
First, you have some time to look at questions 1 to 5.
Now listen carefully and answer questions 1 to 5.

Manager: "Good morning, Greenfield Eco-Resort and Wildlife Sanctuary customer reservations desk. Martin speaking. How may I be of service to you today?"
Marcus: "Good morning, Martin. I am calling from the University of Aberdeen. We wish to confirm an all-inclusive eco-lodge reservation for our wildlife biodiversity research expedition next month."
Manager: "Delighted to assist, sir! We host several university field research teams and scientific expeditions throughout the summer months. May I begin by taking your full surname for the official group file?"
Marcus: "Yes, certainly. It is Abernathy. That is spelled A-B-E-R-N-A-T-H-Y. And my given name is Marcus."
Manager: "Thank you, Dr. Abernathy: A-B-E-R-N-A-T-H-Y. And could I record your primary direct contact telephone number in case of any arrival notifications or emergency liaison out on the wetlands?"
Marcus: "Certainly. You can reach me on 07700 900552... Oh, hold on just a moment! That number is my university department secretary's desk phone in Aberdeen, which won't be manned while we are out in the field. My active direct satellite mobile number is 07700 900331."
Manager: "Right, let me update that in our system immediately: 07700 900331. Perfect. Now, regarding your accommodation style across our grounds: we offer our standard lakeside canvas tents down by the reed beds, or our insulated Timber Chalets situated higher on the wooded ridge overlooking the valley."
Marcus: "Our team initially debated the lakeside canvas tents because of the proximity to the water for amphibian sampling, but because we are carrying highly sensitive audio recording equipment, thermal imaging cameras, and biometric sensors that require temperature control, we definitely need the Timber Chalet."
Manager: "A very wise choice! The Timber Chalet has full climate stabilization, humidity regulation, and lockable equipment storage lockers to protect sensitive electronic gear. And how many nights will your expedition party be residing with us?"
Marcus: "We will be arriving on Monday afternoon and departing on Friday morning, so we will need accommodation for exactly 4 nights."
Manager: "Noted: exactly 4 nights. And lastly, regarding your arrival transport: how will your team be travelling from the regional railway station?"
Marcus: "We are arriving by the morning express train from Glasgow Central and will need resort shuttle transport for our six researchers and their equipment crates."
Manager: "Excellent. We provide a complimentary solar-powered electric shuttle bus for all arriving guests. When you exit the station concourse, please head directly to the commercial transport concourse. The shuttle bus collects our passengers right outside Terminal Bay 4."
Marcus: "Terminal Bay 4 right outside the station. Fantastic, Martin! Thank you so much for arranging this so smoothly."

Now look at questions 6 to 10.
Listen carefully and answer questions 6 to 10.

Manager: "Now, Dr. Abernathy, regarding meal catering and dietary requirements: our package includes three full organic meals daily in the Sanctuary Conservatory Dining Room. Are there any specific dietary restrictions in your party?"
Marcus: "Yes, thank you for checking. Two members of our research team require strictly gluten-free meals due to celiac conditions, and one field assistant is vegetarian."
Manager: "Gluten-free and vegetarian options noted for your catering file. Our kitchen prepares certified allergen-safe meals daily from produce grown in our organic kitchen garden. And regarding laboratory and field workstation requirements: do you require electricity connections in the field station hut near the wetlands?"
Marcus: "Yes, absolutely. We will need 24-hour continuous power connections to recharge our camera traps, ultrasonic acoustic loggers, and water sampling centrifuges overnight."
Manager: "Field station power supply included with dedicated backup battery storage and surge protection. Now, in terms of tariffs: the daily total per researcher is £85, which encompasses lodging, laboratory power access, three daily organic meals, and conservation access permits. For your initial booking confirmation, this gives a total deposit requirement of £170 upon reservation."
Marcus: "A £170 deposit total is completely acceptable and will be billed to our university departmental procurement card. What time can we check into the Timber Chalet on Monday?"
Manager: "Check-in is available from 14:00 PM onwards at the main reception lodge. If your train arrives earlier in the morning, you are most welcome to leave your equipment crates in our secure luggage holding facility while you explore the sanctuary trails and bird observation hides."
Marcus: "14:00 PM check-in. Excellent! Thank you so much for your assistance, Martin. You have made our logistics straightforward."
Manager: "My absolute pleasure, Dr. Abernathy. We look forward to hosting your team and supporting your biodiversity research!"

That is the end of Section 1. You now have half a minute to check your answers.`;

const SECTION_2_SCRIPT = `Section 2. You will hear an orientation guide, Brenda Sterling, giving a talk to new visitors at the Industrial Textile Heritage Museum and Historic Silk Mill.
First, you have some time to look at questions 11 to 15.
Now listen carefully and answer questions 11 to 15.

Brenda: "Welcome, everyone, to the Industrial Textile Heritage Museum and Historic Silk Mill! My name is Brenda Sterling, and I will be guiding you through our six-acre restored eighteenth-century manufacturing complex. This site played a pivotal role in the early British Industrial Revolution, transitioning our regional valley from cottage spinning to automated hydraulic factory production. Over two centuries ago, this river valley resounded with the clatter of hundreds of automated looms that transformed raw imported silk cocoons into luxurious textiles exported across the British Empire.

Before we commence our walking tour through the watermill wheelhouse, please consult the site map printed on your visitor guides. We are currently gathered at Point A, the Central Gateway Courtyard, right in front of the historic clocktower, museum gift shop, and ticket hall. 

Directly to your left, marked as Point B on your plan, is the Watermill Wheelhouse, housing our fully operational fifteen-foot iron waterwheel, constructed in 1784 to power the spinning frames. When the sluice gates open, this immense iron wheel generates over thirty horsepower from the rushing river current, transferring mechanical energy via heavy overhead drive shafts and leather belts to all three upper workshop floors.

If you proceed across the timber footbridge spanning the mill race to Point C, you will enter the Weaving Shed. Here, live demonstrations of traditional handloom weaving and steam-powered Jacquard looms take place every hour on the hour, showcasing the intricate punched cards that prefigured early computing and automated data storage.

Adjacent to the Weaving Shed at Point D is the Dye House & Botanical Garden, where historic natural dyes extracted from madder root, woad, and marigold flowers are cultivated and prepared. Our master dyers demonstrate how wool and silk yarns were colored using purely organic botanicals without artificial chemicals.

Finally, along the canal basin at Point F, stands the Steam Engine Boiler House, featuring a restored 1845 Beam Engine that replaced waterpower during periods of low river flow and drought. Its immense walking beam and copper boilers illustrate the rapid transition from water to coal-fired steam energy."

Now look at questions 16 to 20.
Listen carefully and answer questions 16 to 20.

Brenda: "Now, let me share important practical information regarding museum operating hours, ticket options, and special seasonal exhibitions so you can maximize your time with us today.

Standard adult admission passes cost £12, while university students and senior citizens qualify for our £8 concession pass upon presenting valid identification. Children under seven enter completely free of charge, and family ticket bundles are available at the front desk.

Our museum grounds, outdoor heritage gardens, and exhibition galleries remain open Tuesday through Sunday from 10:00 AM to 17:00 PM. Please note that the main ticket gates close strictly at 16:15 PM to ensure all guests have sufficient time to finish their exhibitions and exit the historic site safely before darkness.

For visitors interested in textile conservation and traditional handicrafts: we host artisan spinning workshops every Wednesday afternoon from 13:30 to 15:30. In these sessions, master craftspeople teach raw wool carding, drop spindle spinning, and treadle wheel operation. Advanced booking at the main reception desk is required, as workshop sizes are strictly capped at twelve participants to guarantee personal attention and ensure adequate equipment for every learner.

Finally, photography using non-flash cameras and personal mobile phones is permitted across all open galleries and outdoor grounds. However, tripods and commercial filming equipment are strictly prohibited inside the historic Weaving Shed due to narrow aisle safety regulations and the close proximity of moving loom shuttles. We also invite you to visit our riverside cafe near the canal wharf for freshly brewed tea and home-baked scones. Thank you for your attention, and enjoy your visit!"

That is the end of Section 2. You now have half a minute to check your answers.`;

const SECTION_3_SCRIPT = `Section 3. You will hear a seminar discussion between Dr. Helena Vance, a senior marine scientist, and two postgraduate research students, Liam and Sophia, discussing their research on marine microplastic contamination and bio-remediation strategies.
First, you have some time to look at questions 21 to 25.
Now listen carefully and answer questions 21 to 25.

Dr. Vance: "Good afternoon, Liam, Sophia. Let us review your collaborative research proposal on marine microplastics and biological remediation strategies before next week's international oceanographic symposium in Edinburgh. Liam, what is the most alarming quantitative finding from your recent neuston-net water sampling data across the North Sea estuarine transects?"

Liam: "Dr. Vance, the spatial density of microplastic particles smaller than five millimeters in diameter exceeded our initial hydrodynamic predictive models by over forty percent. When we analyzed the recovered polymer composition in the spectroscopy laboratory using Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy, secondary microplastics derived from synthetic polyester and nylon textile laundry fibers and photodegrading single-use packaging represented the vast majority of synthetic debris in coastal surface waters. Every single cubic meter of surface seawater sampled in the outer Firth contained between twelve and forty-eight synthetic micro-fragments, with concentrations peaking sharply after heavy autumn storm runoff."

Dr. Vance: "A sobering empirical baseline, Liam. And Sophia, how do these microscopic polymer particles impact lower trophic marine food webs and benthic oceanic organisms?"

Sophia: "Because planktonic filter-feeders such as copepods, euphausiids, and mussel larvae mistake neutrally buoyant microplastics for natural phytoplankton chains, synthetic polymers enter the very base of the marine food chain. Once ingested, endogenous toxic chemical additives like plasticizing phthalates, flame retardants, and bisphenol A leach out and bioaccumulate in lipid fish tissues, escalating in concentration as they move up trophic levels to marine apex predators and ultimately human seafood consumers. This process of trophic biomagnification causes severe endocrine disruption, impaired gonadal development, reduced reproductive fecundity, and chronic hepatic cellular inflammation across marine taxa. Furthermore, hydrophobic microplastic surfaces act as chemical magnets that adsorb persistent organic pollutants such as polychlorinated biphenyls and dissolved heavy metals from ambient coastal water, effectively serving as concentrated toxic delivery vectors directly into fish intestinal linings."

Dr. Vance: "A very thorough summary of the physiological and toxicological vectors. Now, regarding your experimental bio-remediation protocol: in the second phase of your project, you evaluated bacterial enzyme degradation using engineered Ideonella sakaiensis strains inside closed-loop bioreactors in the marine biotechnology laboratory."

Liam: "Yes, Dr. Vance! In our controlled bioreactor trials, modified PETase and MHETase dual-enzyme cascades broke down crystalline polyethylene terephthalate polymer chains into environmentally benign monomer building blocks—specifically terephthalic acid and ethylene glycol—within seventy-two hours under controlled 30-degree Celsius reaction conditions."

Sophia: "Which offers a remarkably promising, eco-friendly industrial pathway for treating municipal wastewater treatment plant effluent and commercial laundry discharge inside filtration tanks before synthetic microplastics ever reach open marine estuaries, reducing particulate polymer loads by an estimated seventy percent at point-source discharge."

Now look at questions 26 to 30.
Listen carefully and answer questions 26 to 30.

Dr. Vance: "Excellent experimental progress indeed. Scaling immobilized enzyme filters at municipal sewage outfalls could be transformative for coastal conservation. Now, let us finalize your individual project task assignments for the final symposium manuscript: writing the experimental methodology section, running the estuarine fluid simulations, authoring the environmental policy analysis, drafting the executive summary, and compiling the bibliography."

Sophia: "Since I supervised the bacterial bioreactor cultures, enzyme assays, and spectrophotometric calibration curves in the laboratory, I will write the experimental methodology section."

Liam: "And I am currently running the three-dimensional computational fluid dynamics models for estuarine tidal dispersion and sediment settling, so I will draft the fluid simulation section."

Sophia: "Regarding the environmental policy analysis on municipal wastewater filtration standards and washing machine micro-fiber trap mandates: since Liam reviewed the European maritime directives and national environmental regulations, he will author that policy section."

Liam: "Which leaves Sophia with compiling the bibliographical references and ensuring full compliance with the international marine biology journal style guide formatting."

Dr. Vance: "And as usual with collaborative postgraduate research publications, you will co-author the executive summary jointly to synthesize your empirical findings and policy recommendations. Make sure your complete manuscript draft is submitted to my department mailbox by Friday noon so that we can conduct a thorough peer review before the Edinburgh conference."

Sophia & Liam: "Understood, Dr. Vance! Thank you for your feedback."

That is the end of Section 3. You now have half a minute to check your answers.`;

const SECTION_4_SCRIPT = `Section 4. You will hear an engineering lecture given by Professor Alistair Sterling from the Department of Civil & Structural Engineering on architectural aerodynamics and wind mitigation in tall building design.
First, you have some time to look at questions 31 to 40.
Now listen carefully and answer questions 31 to 40.

Professor Sterling: "Good morning, students. In today's structural engineering lecture, we shall examine architectural aerodynamics and how innovative structural engineering mitigates extreme aerodynamic wind forces acting on supertall skyscrapers exceeding three hundred meters in height.

As modern metropolitan centers grow vertically to accommodate dense urban populations, structural engineers have discovered that wind—rather than gravity or dead weight—becomes the dominant governing lateral load in tall building design. When high-velocity wind encounters a massive urban structure, it does not simply push against the glass and steel facade in a uniform, static manner; rather, it creates complex fluid dynamic phenomena termed Vortex Shedding. As turbulent atmospheric wind flows around a bluff, rigid rectangular tower, alternating low-pressure swirling vortices detach periodically from opposing leeward corners of the building, inducing violent lateral oscillations perpendicular to the wind direction known as crosswind motion.

If the periodic frequency of this vortex shedding matches the natural structural resonant frequency of the skyscraper, a dangerous physical condition known as aeroelastic lock-in or catastrophic mechanical resonance can occur, amplifying building sway and producing severe motion sickness among upper-floor occupants or even structural fatigue failure. At elevations exceeding three hundred meters within the atmospheric boundary layer, crosswind forces generated by vortex shedding can be up to three times greater than the direct along-wind drag forces pushing against the windward facade.

To counteract vortex shedding at its aerodynamic source, contemporary structural engineers employ sophisticated wind-tunnel modeling and aerodynamic shaping techniques. Rather than constructing monolithic rectangular prisms with sharp 90-degree corners, modern supertall towers incorporate softened rounded corners, slotted corner fins, tapered elevations that narrow toward the spire, and porous aerodynamic cut-through openings—or sky-plazas—that allow high-pressure wind to bleed harmlessly through the core of the structure. For example, the 632-meter Shanghai Tower utilizes a continuous 120-degree spiral twist combined with an asymmetrical tapered profile from base to crown, disorganized vortex formation along the height of the tower and reducing structural wind loads by a remarkable twenty-four percent. This dramatic aerodynamic reduction saved an estimated fifty-eight million dollars in structural steel reinforcement during construction, while dramatically reducing lateral drift under severe Pacific typhoons.

Beyond passive exterior aerodynamic shaping, structural engineers install active and passive auxiliary mechanical dampening systems near the crown of supertall towers. The most celebrated engineering example is the Tuned Mass Damper, universally abbreviated as TMD. A tuned mass damper consists of a colossal pendular steel sphere—frequently weighing over six hundred metric tons, as installed in the Taipei 101 skyscraper—suspended by heavy braided steel cables and connected to viscous hydraulic cylinders inside the upper mechanical floors of the tower. When powerful typhoon winds or seismic ground movements cause the building frame to sway in one direction, inertial forces and computer-controlled hydraulic actuators drive the massive suspended sphere in the exact opposite direction, absorbing kinetic energy through hydraulic damping and stabilizing the tower frame by up to forty percent.

In addition to solid spherical pendulums, engineers increasingly deploy tuned slosh dampers—massive baffled rectangular water tanks positioned on penthouse mechanical levels. These tanks are mathematically tuned so that the sloshing water oscillates at the exact same resonant frequency as the tower, dissipating lateral wind forces through viscous fluid drag against internal baffles while simultaneously serving as high-elevation auxiliary emergency firefighting water reserves. Other cutting-edge towers employ aerodynamic corner chamfers, stepped helical setbacks, as seen in the Burj Khalifa where every setback tier is oriented to "confuse the wind," preventing organized low-pressure eddies from synchronizing along the vertical axis.

In conclusion, as urban land scarcity and sustainable high-density zoning drive buildings ever higher into the planetary boundary layer, wind engineering stands at the vital intersection of architectural elegance and structural survival. Modern skyscrapers must be designed not as rigid, immovable concrete monoliths fighting against nature, but as aerodynamically sculpted instruments that harmonize with atmospheric fluid dynamics. Thank you for your attention."

That is the end of Section 4. You now have one minute to check your answers.`;

