  task1Prompt: "The process diagram shows how recycled glass bottles are collected, cleaned, crushed, melted, and manufactured into new beverage containers. Summarise the process.",
  task1Model: `The flowchart illustrates the sequential industrial process by which discarded glass bottles are collected, recycled, and transformed into brand-new beverage containers.

Overall, the recycling cycle comprises six primary stages, beginning with the collection and sorting of post-consumer glass waste, proceeding through physical cleaning and thermal melting, and culminating in the automated molding of fresh bottles ready for distribution.

In the initial stage, consumers deposit waste bottles into municipal recycling receptacles, after which dedicated collection vehicles transport the glass to specialized recycling facilities. Upon arrival, the bottles undergo rigorous high-pressure water washing to eliminate residual contaminants, labels, and adhesives. Subsequently, the cleaned glass is transferred to an industrial crushing unit, where heavy rollers pulverize the containers into fine glass granules known as cullet.

Following mechanical crushing, the cullet is blended with raw sand and soda ash before entering a high-temperature furnace operating at approximately 1,500 degrees Celsius. Once fully liquefied, molten glass streams are fed into precision blow-molding machines that shape the liquid into newly formed bottles. Finally, after gradual thermal cooling, the manufactured containers are inspected, packed, and dispatched to beverage bottling plants, completing the circular manufacturing loop.`,
  modelEssay: `The ubiquitous proliferation of digital telecommunications has radically altered the topography of human interpersonal interaction across modern civilization. While technological enthusiasts argue that connectivity platforms enrich relationships across geographical boundaries, critics maintain that screen-mediated socializing fosters superficiality and acute emotional alienation. In my assessment, while digital tools offer unprecedented communicative convenience, they frequently dilute the depth and emotional resonance of interpersonal bonds when substituted for real-world intimacy.

On one hand, digital technology undeniably expands the perimeter of human connectivity. Long-distance familial and collegial relationships, historically constrained by geographical dislocation, can now be maintained instantaneously via high-definition video conferencing and instant messaging. Moreover, specialized online communities empower marginalized individuals—such as rare disease sufferers or neurodivergent youths—to discover vital peer validation and emotional solidarity that may be absent in their immediate physical neighborhoods. In professional spheres, collaborative platforms enable cross-continental teamwork, creating inclusive scholarly and commercial networks.

On the other hand, digital communication inherently sacrifices the multisensory nuance essential for genuine human bonding. In-person dialogue relies heavily upon subconscious nonverbal cues: micro-expressions, tonal inflections, eye contact, and physical presence. In contrast, text-based and algorithmic interactions frequently encourage transactional, performative communication centered on social media validation and curated persona curation. Empirical sociological surveys reveal that despite being nominally connected to thousands of online contacts, modern youths report unprecedented levels of loneliness and communicative anxiety when required to engage in spontaneous face-to-face discourse.

In conclusion, although telecommunication technologies eliminate geographic barriers and facilitate functional information exchange, over-reliance upon digital platforms risks substituting authentic psychological intimacy with curated, superficial interactions. Technology should therefore serve as a complement to, rather than an outright replacement for, authentic physical presence and heartfelt personal dialogue.`,
